WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.370
Fans had been waiting for what felt like an
eternity for a Wonder Woman movie, so when

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:09.320
it finally arrived, it was a worldwide phenomenon
that made Gal Gadot a superstar and set a

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.540
new standard for DC’s movie universe.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.900
Needless to say, kids loved it, but when they
watch it with their parents, the adults in

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.240
the room latch onto a few things that children
might not pick up on.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.900
From the quirks of Themysciran society to
Chris Pine's love of a certain mode of transport,

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.920
here are the things only adults notice in
Wonder Woman.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.920
Secrets and wars

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.500
Before it dives into an adventure set against
the backdrop of World War I, the film opens

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.670
with Diana receiving a package from Bruce
Wayne containing a photo which proved to him

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.740
that Diana had been around, looking exactly
the same, for decades.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.820
Bruce tracked the original down and sent it
to Diana as a gesture of friendship to help

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.070
her keep her secret, and we later learn the
photo was taken in the village of Veld after

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.590
Diana, Steve, and their compatriots liberated
the town from German soldiers, and apparently

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.040
passed into history later without anyone ever
taking too much notice.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.760
But let's unpack this for a moment: A photographer
captured this image, featuring a lady with

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.840
a glowing rope who'd just taken an entire
town back from the Germans, and then it just…went

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:08.340
away?

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.340
No one followed up?

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.000
No one ever asked the photographer what was
up with the literal Amazon hanging out with

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:13.660
soldiers?

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:15.320
Well, maybe someone did.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.280
There are plenty of legends of supernatural
events occuring during World War I, including

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.100
tales of angelic figures who helped defend
the Belgian city of Mons.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.420
Diana certainly fits the bill of an angelic
figure who showed up to help soldiers, so

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.940
maybe she was just written off as a legend…
who just happened to have her picture taken.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.260
Selective understanding

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.660
The film’s greatest source of comedy is
the culture clash that ensues as Steve Trevor

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.630
tries to introduce Diana to modern life in
1917, and her difficulty understanding it.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:43.860
Not that we can blame her.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.940
Things get pretty complicated when Steve has
to explain the legal concept of a marriage

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.150
that’s permanent until it isn’t, for instance.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.340
“Then why do they do it?

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.560
I have no idea”

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.640
If marriage is that difficult to understand,
though, it stands to reason that she’s going

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.500
to have a hard time with the rest of the world.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.390
Diana stays in our world after the events
of the movie, which means she suddenly has

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.940
to learn about everything from banking to
home ownership.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.380
Imagine the conversation that ensues the first
time someone has to explain bankruptcy or

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:11.650
parking tickets to her.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.260
Of course, if the film stopped to acknowledge
every single explanation that Steve and Etta

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.740
had to offer, Ares probably would’ve conquered
the world without much trouble.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.640
There are a few other examples that are pretty
notable, however.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.220
“What’s THAT?”

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.140
“It’s a… uh..”

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.360
On the boat from Themyscira, Diana makes it
clear that she has an understanding of reproduction,

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:38.270
but is puzzled by the sight of a couple holding
hands.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.430
Themyscira is rich in art and culture, but
Diana doesn't seem to understand the purpose

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.800
of clothes that exist for anything other than
fighting.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.280
She views ice cream as a true miracle, yet
has no trouble with the concept of a camera.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.400
Bullets and bracelets

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.780
It's established pretty early on that Wonder
Woman has powers other Amazon warriors do

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.960
not, and by the end of the movie, we know
why.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.689
Diana isn’t just an Amazon, she’s a demigod,
the daughter of Zeus and Hippolyta, giving

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:05.720
her powers far beyond the warrior prowess
of a typical Amazon.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:10.080
These powers apparently include jumping to
incredible heights, supernatural toughness,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:10.880
and super-strength.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.459
We see her do things like walking out of a
collapsing tower unscathed, and tossing around

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.500
armored vehicles on more than one occasion.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.640
Yet whenever anyone fires a gun at her, she
deflects it either with her bracelets or with

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.320
her shield.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.120
If Diana can walk away from being tossed around
by the literal God of War, there's a good

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.950
chance bullets won't do much against her,
but the film never shows us that.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.129
In the comics, it’s mentioned that she’s
so skilled that she can control where they

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:37.749
ricochet, but in the movie, we’re guessing
it’s because deflecting slow-mo bullets

00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:38.880
just looks really cool.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.760
There’s another reason, though: at the beginning
of the film, she watches all of her Amazon

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.800
sisters die in a hail of gunfire.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.860
Maybe it's not that Diana can't survive bullets,
but that she just doesn't want to try.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.900
Motorcycle madness

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.439
If you go to see a movie starring Chris Pine,
chances are pretty good that you’re going

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.599
to see him on a motorcycle.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.400
He even rode one in the distant future of
the Star Trek films, including the one in

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.739
which he had to find a motorcycle on an alien
planet.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.940
If Disney had let him, he probably would have
done it in Into the Woods, too.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.459
World War I makes for a much easier place
to find a motorcycle than the 24th century,

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.280
but it still feels a little shoehorned in.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.620
After Diana and Steve head to the village
of Veld on horses to investigate Dr. Poison’s

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:20.480
chemical weapons, Diana flies into a rage
and hops back on her horse to pursue Ludendorff.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:24.620
Steve gets to the village moments after Diana,
but instead of mounting up to join her, he

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:28.940
spends a few moments looking for a motorcycle
instead, even though his horse is still standing

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:29.700
right there.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:35.740
Maybe Steve only rides horses as a last resort,
or maybe Pine wanted to look cool on a motorcycle

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:36.750
again.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:37.500
And honestly, can you blame him?

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:38.560
Sharp-dressed

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.670
When Etta Candy and Steve Trevor first try
to dress Diana in modern clothes, Steve slips

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.780
a pair of glasses on her, and Etta makes the
a pretty obvious observation about that disguise:

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.360
“Suddenly she’s not the most beautiful
woman you’ve ever seen”

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.980
Glasses or no, Diana stands out in a crowd,
and when she and Steve infiltrate a German

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.530
party, it’s understandably difficult for
anyone in the crowd to take their eyes off

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.020
of her.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.080
Her goal is to kill Ludendorff, and she aims
to do so by stuffing her sword down the back

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.180
of her dress until it's time for some stabbing.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:08.480
It's a great visual, but it doesn’t quite
hold up if you take a minute to think about

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:08.980
it.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:13.040
With the number of people taking second looks
at Diana as she walks past, someone probably

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.050
should’ve noticed she was carrying a giant
piece of magic metal.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.540
Could they have seen the hilt of the sword
and simply thought it was elaborate jewelry?

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.850
Sure, but Ludendorff probably should’ve
noticed something was up when he literally

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.000
put his hands on it.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.140
Leftover weapons

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.710
Diana spends the film convinced that all she
has to do to stop the war is kill Ares, but

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:31.960
Steve has a different mission.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.840
His goal is the same, but he’s going after
General Ludendorff and Doctor Poison, who

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.560
have manufactured extremely powerful chemical
weapons.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.140
The evil doctor didn't just make the poison
gas that will kill untold numbers of people

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.370
if it's unleashed on the world, though — she
also granted Ludendorff incredible strength

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.720
and durability,which almost makes him Diana’s
equal in combat.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:51.500
Almost.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.800
At the end of the film, Ares tries to get
Diana to kill Doctor Poison, who he considers

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.920
the ultimate example of humanity's corruption.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:02.320
Diana spares her, but her lab is destroyed
by Steve and his team, and the poison gas

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.680
is neutralized when Steve sacrifices his life
to blow it all up.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.900
It all seems like a pretty tidy ending, except
that we know Doctor Poison worked in multiple

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.460
places to perfect her formula.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:15.400
Research like this tends to involve a lot
of documentation, and it makes sense that

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.470
she’d even have earlier, slightly less deadly
versions of both the poison and the strength

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:20.940
enhancer that we never saw.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.220
Even assuming she never made a chemical weapon
again after Diana spared her, it's not hard

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.890
to imagine Doctor Poison’s creations, or
the science behind them, falling into the

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.220
hands of the Nazis just a few years later.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:32.860
Gods and monsters

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:38.440
The film's climactic showdown between Diana
and Ares reveals a twist: Ares was not Ludendorff,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.360
as Diana had assumed, but was actually Sir
Patrick Morgan, who’s been manipulating

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.720
events for ages in order to give humanity
the right machines of war to destroy themselves.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:51.360
In a battle with the unmasked Ares, the God
of War seems to have telekinetic command over

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.720
just about everything in sight.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:57.520
He uses that to fashion himself some fierce-looking,
comic accurate scrap metal armor, and hurl

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.740
shards of airplanes and tanks at our heroine.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.700
Those are very cool things for him to do,
but as the battle progresses, he reaches into

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.460
the skies and… summons lightning bolts.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.500
The movie can give its gods whatever powers
it wants, but isn't lightning Zeus' thing?

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.080
And don’t we know that because we see him
using it in a flashback in this very same

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:15.600
film?

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.340
Why does the the power that Ares has over
weapons of war, which makes perfect sense,

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:21.740
suddenly extend to lightning?

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.500
Sure, it definitely looks cool, but viewers
with a very basic understanding of mythology

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.240
might have a hard time figuring that one out.

